<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Prussia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was the easternmost part of Prussia on the south-eastern Baltic coast, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>. It grew from the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> left after 1466, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> in 1525 and the core of the later Kingdom of Prussia. Separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-corridor">Polish Corridor</a> after 1919, it was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-prussia.jpg" alt="East Prussia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Preu%C3%9Fen_-_Provinz_Ostpreu%C3%9Fen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The landscape of East Prussia was gently rolling plains and small hills, flatter in the north and with more hills in the south, notably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masuria">Masuria</a>. In the north-west the province bordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, from which the Vistula Spit and the Curonian Spit separated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a> and the Curonian Lagoon. Most of the province's rivers, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pregolya">Pregel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a>, emptied into the lagoons. Its people included Germans, Masurians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in the south and Lithuanian-speaking Prussians in the north-east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 13th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> took possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and created a monastic state to administer the conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a>. In 1525 Grand Master Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, secularised the Prussian branch of the Order and became the first duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>. The duchy passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1618, and in 1701 Elector Frederick III crowned himself King in Prussia.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Ducal Prussia</a>, enlarged by Warmia, was designated East Prussia by decree of 31 January 1773. By the Treaty of Tilsit of 9 July 1807 Prussia lost almost half its territory. Between 1829 and 1878 the province was joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Russian Army invaded in 1914 and was defeated at Tannenberg in 1914 and the Second Battle of the Masurian Lakes in 1915. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> made the province an exclave, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> annexed the Memel Territory in 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After Nazi Germany's defeat in 1945 East Prussia was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> under the Potsdam Conference. The northern part became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad Oblast</a> of the Russian republic in 1946, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> was renamed Kaliningrad, and the Klaipėda Region went to the Lithuanian republic. Most of the German population left in the evacuation at the end of the war, and an estimated 300,000 died in the bombing, the fighting and through mistreatment, hunger, cold and disease. The remainder were expelled in accordance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “East Prussia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the First Partition of Poland in 1772 the former duchy, with Warmia, was reorganized as the Province of <b>East Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its density, 130.7 inhabitants per square kilometre, hid large contrasts: 60.9 in <b>East Prussia</b> against 295.6 in the Rhine Province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The capture of the Klaipėda Region, detached from <b>East Prussia</b>, during the Ruhr Crisis of 1923, and its autonomy within Lithuania from May 1924, set it against Germany, whose inhabitants of the region resisted Lithuanian rule in the 1930s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Samland became part of the duchy, and its lands later lay in <b>East Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-samland">Bishopric of Samland</a></li></ul></section>
